WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.660
So what if I told you that the math making your laptop physically hot right now is the

00:07.660 --> 00:13.560
exact same geometry that forced ancient humans to change how they conjugate their verbs?

00:13.960 --> 00:16.500
I mean, it sounds completely absurd on its face, right?

00:16.520 --> 00:17.120
It really does.

00:17.560 --> 00:22.920
But today, we are taking a deep dive into a theory that claims to have found basically

00:22.920 --> 00:24.600
the universal source code.

00:24.760 --> 00:24.940
Yeah.

00:25.240 --> 00:29.740
And usually, you know, when we think about the rules governing our reality, we put them

00:29.740 --> 00:31.620
into these completely separate airtight boxes.

00:31.900 --> 00:32.120
Mm-hmm.

00:32.420 --> 00:32.860
Absolutely.

00:33.000 --> 00:34.040
We compartmentalize everything.

00:34.200 --> 00:34.360
Right.

00:34.620 --> 00:36.020
There's the box for quantum physics.

00:36.220 --> 00:37.960
There's the box for electrical engineering.

00:38.060 --> 00:41.860
And then, like, way over on the other side of the building, there's the box for cognitive

00:41.860 --> 00:43.440
neuroscience and human culture.

00:43.560 --> 00:43.800
Yeah.

00:43.860 --> 00:48.000
We just assume that the math used to design a silicon microchip has absolutely nothing

00:48.000 --> 00:51.080
to do with, say, the evolution of the English language.

00:51.180 --> 00:51.660
Exactly.

00:51.960 --> 00:52.160
Yeah.

00:52.160 --> 00:56.240
But that assumption is exactly what we are challenging in this deep dive today.

00:56.240 --> 01:03.680
We are looking at a massive and, I mean, wildly ambitious stack of academic manuscripts from

01:03.680 --> 01:05.300
an independent researcher named Flixia.

01:05.600 --> 01:07.420
And when we say massive, we're not kidding.

01:07.620 --> 01:12.500
I mean, we are looking at papers covering everything from the thermodynamics of logic gates

01:12.500 --> 01:20.040
to the neurobiology of consciousness, all the way to the geometric modeling of linguistic drift.

01:20.180 --> 01:21.000
It's staggering.

01:21.260 --> 01:21.540
It is.

01:21.700 --> 01:28.100
The mission for us today is to really untangle this single mind-bending mathematical thread

01:28.100 --> 01:32.400
that Flixian claims sews every single one of these fields together.

01:32.400 --> 01:36.600
And that thread is a framework Flixian calls continuation geometry.

01:36.820 --> 01:37.080
Right.

01:37.580 --> 01:42.680
Which is heavily tied to something called the RSVP framework, the relativistic scalar vector

01:42.680 --> 01:43.040
plenum.

01:43.140 --> 01:43.360
Right.

01:43.600 --> 01:48.580
I have to admit, when I first started reading this for our deep dive, I just felt this sheer

01:48.580 --> 01:50.360
enthusiastic bewilderment.

01:50.460 --> 01:51.580
Oh, I totally get that.

01:51.620 --> 01:52.640
It's a lot to take in.

01:52.740 --> 01:57.500
I mean, the sheer ambition to claim that a resistor on a circuit board and a thought in your cerebral

01:57.500 --> 02:02.580
cortex are executing the exact same mathematical operation, it sounds like pure science fiction

02:02.580 --> 02:02.920
to me.

02:03.160 --> 02:05.780
It definitely reads like science fiction at first glance, I'll give you that.

02:05.960 --> 02:11.300
But, and this is crucial, the foundational math is actually deeply rooted in established,

02:11.560 --> 02:13.020
rigorously proven theories.

02:13.360 --> 02:13.560
Right.

02:13.620 --> 02:14.440
It's not just made up.

02:14.540 --> 02:14.880
Exactly.

02:15.660 --> 02:20.200
Flixian is drawing very heavily on the work of Tristan Needham, specifically his textbook

02:20.200 --> 02:21.800
Visual Complex Analysis.

02:22.120 --> 02:22.360
Okay.

02:22.360 --> 02:29.380
What Flixian has done is take Needham's incredibly elegant way of visualizing complex math and

02:29.380 --> 02:33.400
just push those concepts to their absolute furthest limits.

02:33.760 --> 02:40.180
So, to even begin to understand how Flixian's universe works, we actually have to go all

02:40.180 --> 02:41.200
the way back to the basics.

02:41.520 --> 02:42.080
Right?

02:42.180 --> 02:45.300
Like, we have to fix how we look at numbers themselves.

02:45.400 --> 02:46.100
We really do.

02:46.200 --> 02:49.800
We essentially have to completely relearn how to read the language of mathematics.

02:49.800 --> 02:54.760
Which sounds daunting, but there is this fantastic entry point for this in our sources.

02:55.100 --> 03:00.500
It's actually a piece of correspondence regarding Flixian's own handwritten math notation.

03:00.620 --> 03:01.280
Oh, yeah.

03:01.400 --> 03:02.420
This is a great story.

03:02.620 --> 03:02.760
Yeah.

03:02.840 --> 03:07.540
So, someone was trying to transcribe Flixian's handwritten notes into standard digital text,

03:07.740 --> 03:09.000
and they hit a complete wall.

03:09.240 --> 03:14.500
They saw this symbol and thought Flixian was just writing a sloppy tilde, you know, that little

03:14.500 --> 03:17.900
squiggly line we use in math to mean approximately equal to.

03:17.940 --> 03:18.140
Right.

03:18.200 --> 03:19.040
Like, close enough.

03:19.040 --> 03:19.760
Exactly.

03:20.460 --> 03:23.960
But Flixian immediately corrected them, and it completely blew my mind why.

03:24.520 --> 03:30.080
Well, the transcriber was just silently normalizing these symbols into standard asymptotic equivalents,

03:30.480 --> 03:33.580
just assuming Flixian meant A is roughly equal to B.

03:33.820 --> 03:38.260
But Flixian was deliberately using custom, highly specific symbols.

03:38.480 --> 03:43.760
One of them looked like sideways square brackets facing each other, and another looked like back-to-back

03:43.760 --> 03:44.800
rounded parentheses.

03:44.800 --> 03:48.060
And the distinction there was critical, right?

03:48.280 --> 03:50.760
Because Flixian wasn't talking about mere approximations.

03:50.920 --> 03:51.540
Not at all.

03:51.680 --> 03:54.060
This wasn't a casual, close enough situation.

03:54.680 --> 03:59.420
Flixian was inventing a totally new notation for the infinitesimal limit.

03:59.420 --> 04:01.720
Okay, let's unpack this for everyone listening.

04:02.180 --> 04:07.040
Because, I mean, what does it actually mean to invent a new notation for the infinitesimal limit?

04:07.340 --> 04:09.180
Well, think about how we usually treat algebra.

04:09.560 --> 04:13.840
We treat it as an abstract game of moving letters around an equal sign, right?

04:14.000 --> 04:14.660
Yeah, exactly.

04:14.660 --> 04:19.320
But for Flixian, math is a description of physical geometric actions.

04:20.180 --> 04:23.920
So when you look at two curves on a graph that are getting closer and closer together,

04:24.380 --> 04:28.380
standard math just says, you know, as they go to infinity, they are basically the same.

04:28.580 --> 04:28.680
Right.

04:29.120 --> 04:36.240
Flixian's sideways brackets are a geometric notation showing how finite measurable increments in space

04:36.240 --> 04:42.840
actually become their differential counterparts when you zoom in to the infinitesimal level.

04:42.840 --> 04:48.400
So it's not just that they're close, it's a meticulous obsession with geometry over casual algebra.

04:48.720 --> 04:49.280
Precisely.

04:49.640 --> 04:56.480
When Flixian says A is ultimately equal to B, it means that at the deepest, most microscopic limit of reality,

04:57.040 --> 05:01.720
the literal geometric shape of A and the geometric shape of B perfectly align.

05:01.840 --> 05:02.140
Wow.

05:02.400 --> 05:06.740
And that distinction, that really sets the stage for the core concept of this entire deep dive.

05:06.800 --> 05:07.260
It does.

05:07.400 --> 05:10.960
Because if math is always describing a physical geometric action,

05:10.960 --> 05:13.980
then we have to completely rethink the numbers we learned in high school.

05:14.300 --> 05:16.200
Specifically, this idea of the unsquared number.

05:16.380 --> 05:16.600
Yes.

05:16.920 --> 05:18.280
The unsquared number is key here.

05:18.560 --> 05:20.200
I love this concept so much.

05:20.280 --> 05:22.160
So think back to algebra class, right?

05:22.740 --> 05:25.820
We are all taught about imaginary numbers.

05:26.260 --> 05:27.900
Specifically, the letter I.

05:28.000 --> 05:29.960
Everyone's favorite confusing math topic.

05:30.000 --> 05:30.400
Exactly.

05:30.880 --> 05:34.740
We are told that I is the square root of negative one.

05:34.740 --> 05:39.760
Which, I don't know about you, but on its face, that always felt like a hack to me.

05:39.940 --> 05:40.480
Oh, totally.

05:40.520 --> 05:42.840
Like, you can't multiply a number by itself and get a negative.

05:43.180 --> 05:44.980
A positive times a positive is positive.

05:45.100 --> 05:46.380
A negative times a negative is positive.

05:46.760 --> 05:53.440
So mathematicians just, like, invented this magical imaginary unit to make their equations balance out.

05:53.440 --> 06:00.500
And that feeling, that feeling that it's a hack, is exactly what Tristan Needham and now Fliction are directly addressing.

06:01.180 --> 06:03.520
We've been looking at the letter entirely wrong.

06:03.740 --> 06:07.540
It is not a number in the traditional sense of counting apples or measuring a distance.

06:07.540 --> 06:09.520
It is a geometric operator.

06:09.860 --> 06:12.840
It is a literal instruction to twist space.

06:13.420 --> 06:14.720
An instruction to twist space.

06:14.820 --> 06:15.900
Okay, you've got to walk me through that.

06:15.960 --> 06:19.160
Okay, imagine you are standing on a number line, right at the number one.

06:19.220 --> 06:20.180
You are facing forward.

06:20.340 --> 06:20.720
Got it.

06:20.720 --> 06:28.780
Multiplying your position by I doesn't just change your quantity, it rotates you 90 degrees off that flat number line into a new dimension.

06:29.000 --> 06:30.240
So now I'm facing sideways.

06:30.500 --> 06:32.480
Right, so now you are standing at 1 times I.

06:32.800 --> 06:33.740
You are looking sideways.

06:33.740 --> 06:37.580
Now, if you multiply by I again, that is another 90 degree twist.

06:37.800 --> 06:41.060
Ah, so now I'm facing entirely backward, pointing at negative 1.

06:41.260 --> 06:41.700
Exactly.

06:42.360 --> 06:45.340
The negative 1 didn't come from a magical abstract property.

06:45.660 --> 06:48.880
It came from two consecutive 90 degree spatial rotations.

06:49.240 --> 06:50.460
That makes so much more sense.

06:50.720 --> 06:53.300
This is what Flickshen means by an unsquared number.

06:53.820 --> 07:00.500
An imaginary number is a geometric torsion, a literal twist hiding in the disguise of an algebraic variable.

07:00.500 --> 07:04.500
Which brings us to the most critical word in this entire framework, the Amplitwist.

07:05.420 --> 07:06.500
Yes, the Amplitwist.

07:07.320 --> 07:12.500
If we understand this, we essentially understand the engine of Flickshen's entire universe.

07:12.500 --> 07:17.220
Every complex number does two things to space at the exact same time.

07:17.560 --> 07:21.440
It scales the space, that's the amplitude, and it rotates the space.

07:21.880 --> 07:22.560
That's the twist.

07:22.600 --> 07:23.100
That's it.

07:23.220 --> 07:24.040
Amplitude and twist.

07:24.380 --> 07:26.420
So here is how I visualize it.

07:26.460 --> 07:27.820
You tell me if I'm tracking this correctly.

07:28.000 --> 07:30.320
Imagine you are holding your smartphone, right?

07:30.440 --> 07:32.080
And you're looking at a digital map.

07:32.280 --> 07:32.540
Okay.

07:32.540 --> 07:37.560
You want to look at a specific neighborhood, but the map is sideways and it's way too zoomed out.

07:37.780 --> 07:39.840
What do you physically do with your hand?

07:40.000 --> 07:40.780
You pinch and turn.

07:41.020 --> 07:41.480
Exactly.

07:41.760 --> 07:48.700
You put two fingers on the screen, you pull them apart to zoom in, and at the exact same time, you twist your wrist to rotate the map.

07:49.100 --> 07:53.220
You're scaling and rotating simultaneously in one fluid motion.

07:53.220 --> 08:01.820
So is Flickshen saying that this literal pinch and twist action on your phone screen is the fundamental mathematical language of reality?

08:02.160 --> 08:05.660
That analogy perfectly captures the 2D version of it, yes.

08:05.740 --> 08:08.540
But obviously the universe doesn't live on a flat phone screen.

08:08.660 --> 08:08.860
Right.

08:08.960 --> 08:10.760
It's 3D or 4D with time.

08:10.880 --> 08:11.360
Exactly.

08:11.680 --> 08:17.400
So Flickshen takes that 2D pinch and twist and generalizes it into the four dimensions of space-time.

08:17.700 --> 08:21.860
And to escape the flat plane, the math relies on something called Clifford Algebras.

08:21.860 --> 08:23.560
Okay, Clifford Algebras.

08:23.720 --> 08:25.120
That sounds incredibly intimidating.

08:25.280 --> 08:27.700
How does a pinch and twist escape a flat plane using that?

08:28.040 --> 08:39.120
I know it sounds dense, but without getting bogged down in the massive equations, just think of a Clifford Algebra as a mathematical toolkit that allows you to multiply directions together.

08:39.360 --> 08:40.040
Multiply directions.

08:40.160 --> 08:40.400
Yeah.

08:40.560 --> 08:43.120
On your phone screen, you only have up, down, and left, right.

08:43.120 --> 08:52.500
A Clifford Algebra lets you take that 2D rotation, add a third dimension of depth and a fourth dimension of time, and basically create a single mathematical object.

08:52.820 --> 08:53.800
Like a field operator.

08:54.020 --> 08:54.520
Exactly.

08:54.740 --> 08:58.740
A field operator that can scale and twist in all of those directions at once.

08:58.740 --> 09:04.840
The idea is that reality itself is not made of static objects sitting in some empty void.

09:05.440 --> 09:12.700
Reality is a continuous flowing field that is constantly scaling, flowing, and twisting itself into new configurations.

09:13.040 --> 09:13.260
Wow.

09:13.520 --> 09:18.100
It turns the universe into this dynamic origami constantly folding and unfolding itself.

09:18.300 --> 09:19.520
That's a great way to picture it.

09:19.520 --> 09:22.280
But let's put this beautiful image to the test, right?

09:22.320 --> 09:27.960
Because if this amplit list is really the fundamental language of reality, how does it handle the heavy lifting?

09:28.360 --> 09:32.280
Let's take it into section 2 of our sources, which is quantum mechanics.

09:32.440 --> 09:32.600
Right.

09:32.660 --> 09:33.560
The ultimate test.

09:33.560 --> 09:38.640
How does this apply to the most rigorous fundamental physics we have?

09:38.860 --> 09:44.420
Well, that hurdle is addressed head-on in a paper titled Rotation Before Number.

09:44.420 --> 09:52.960
And to understand the solution, we first have to acknowledge a massive elephant-in-the-room problem with the way we currently do quantum mechanics.

09:53.160 --> 09:53.660
Oh, do tell.

09:53.860 --> 10:00.060
The paper argues that standard quantum math is computationally perfect, but it is epistemically opaque.

10:00.780 --> 10:08.880
Epistemically opaque, which is a very polite academic way of saying it gives us the absolute right answers, but completely hides the why.

10:09.100 --> 10:10.400
That is exactly it.

10:10.460 --> 10:12.500
It is the ultimate trap of abstraction.

10:12.500 --> 10:20.380
Generations of physicists have inherited this incredibly powerful notation things like Schrodinger's equation and Hilbert spaces.

10:20.600 --> 10:22.760
Now, remind us real quick, what is a Hilbert space?

10:22.900 --> 10:27.880
A Hilbert space is basically just a mathematical arena with infinite dimensions where quantum states live.

10:28.060 --> 10:33.500
And all of this math is heavily reliant on complex numbers, on that imaginary unit that we just talked about.

10:33.840 --> 10:35.600
And it works perfectly in the lab.

10:35.600 --> 10:47.420
I mean, we can predict particle behavior to absurd decimal places, but because it's written in the compressed language of algebra, we've lost the geometric intuition that generated it in the first book.

10:47.480 --> 10:48.360
Compressed geometry.

10:48.840 --> 10:53.820
The sources use that phrase, and it really stuck with the complex algebra, is just compressed geometry.

10:54.020 --> 10:54.280
Yes.

10:54.280 --> 11:02.340
When a physicist sees an eye in Schrodinger's equation, they just treat it as a mathematical necessity to make the wave function work.

11:02.960 --> 11:07.420
But Flixson is saying, no, that eye is a geometric quarter turn hiding in plain sight.

11:07.580 --> 11:12.900
It is a literal instruction for spatial torsion that got compressed into a single letter.

11:12.900 --> 11:17.040
So to prove this, the source material does something totally wild.

11:17.440 --> 11:21.980
It completely rebuilds quantum mechanics from scratch without using any complex numbers.

11:22.180 --> 11:23.880
Yes, which is a massive undertaking.

11:24.140 --> 11:27.380
It translates the whole thing into pure, uncompressed real numbers.

11:28.380 --> 11:33.040
But I have a major pushback here, and I'm sure anyone who has studied math is thinking the same thing.

11:33.360 --> 11:42.260
If you just swap complex numbers out and force everything into real numbers, don't you lose the extra dimensions that the imaginary numbers were holding onto?

11:42.260 --> 11:50.020
You absolutely would lose information, yes, unless you expand the coordinate space to explicitly account for those rotations.

11:50.460 --> 11:54.940
And Flixson does exactly this by introducing the real flag space QM.

11:55.360 --> 12:03.160
It's a system where that imaginary unit is entirely replaced by a literal physical quarter turn matrix, which they call JF.

12:03.720 --> 12:05.440
Okay, a physical quarter turn matrix.

12:05.600 --> 12:06.900
How do we visualize that?

12:07.240 --> 12:10.940
Okay, to visualize this, imagine a flagpole planted in the ground.

12:10.940 --> 12:15.040
The position of the pole represents the probability of a quantum particle.

12:15.840 --> 12:22.140
But in quantum mechanics, particles also have a hidden wave-like property that interferes with other particles.

12:22.300 --> 12:23.500
Right, the weird wavy stuff.

12:23.720 --> 12:24.120
Exactly.

12:24.360 --> 12:27.780
So in the flag space, you don't just track the pole, you attach a flag to it.

12:28.000 --> 12:32.900
The 2D flag system tracks not just where the pole is, but the exact geometric angle the flag is pointing.

12:32.920 --> 12:33.500
Oh, that makes sense.

12:33.500 --> 12:46.000
So instead of a magical abstract number that mathematically flips signs in an equation somewhere, you have a literal matrix that physically rotates the flag around the pole by 90 degrees.

12:46.000 --> 12:51.320
So the complex algebra reappears purely as real geometric matrix operations.

12:52.020 --> 12:54.520
The geometry is finally uncompressed and visible.

12:54.660 --> 12:55.740
We can see the flag turning.

12:55.900 --> 12:56.320
Exactly.

12:56.640 --> 13:00.260
But the sources note that this introduces a massive new tension, right?

13:00.780 --> 13:08.620
When you do this uncompressing, especially when you start looking at composite systems, like multiple quantum particles interacting and entangling with each other,

13:08.900 --> 13:12.660
this expanded real number space requires way more parameters.

13:12.900 --> 13:13.440
It does.

13:13.440 --> 13:14.740
Because the math suddenly balloons.

13:14.980 --> 13:15.180
Right.

13:15.620 --> 13:21.980
A complex space might require eight parameters to describe a system, but this uncompressed real space requires 16 parameters.

13:22.080 --> 13:22.600
Yeah, it doubles.

13:22.980 --> 13:24.940
Which leads to my biggest question about this whole theory.

13:25.420 --> 13:27.740
What does this actually mean for reality?

13:28.640 --> 13:35.660
If uncompressing the math suddenly creates states and dimensions that don't actually exist in the laboratory, like things we can never measure,

13:36.320 --> 13:38.480
aren't we just confusing our map for the territory?

13:38.480 --> 13:44.960
We've built this highly detailed map, but it's showing 16 roads when the real world only has eight.

13:45.080 --> 13:49.720
That is the crucial defining question of this entire section, really.

13:50.140 --> 13:52.960
And it brings us to the concept of the admissibility quotient.

13:53.660 --> 13:58.100
You are completely right that the flag space math creates states that look mathematically distinct on paper,

13:58.280 --> 14:02.560
but are totally indistinguishable to any physical measuring device in a lab.

14:02.560 --> 14:03.020
Right.

14:03.100 --> 14:05.760
They look different in the math, but act the same in reality.

14:05.900 --> 14:06.200
Exactly.

14:06.660 --> 14:09.640
And fliction recognizes this over-parameterization.

14:10.380 --> 14:13.080
The solution is that we have to mathematically quotient,

14:13.540 --> 14:16.700
which basically just means filter out those representational artifacts.

14:16.860 --> 14:17.360
Filter them out.

14:17.540 --> 14:17.780
Yeah.

14:17.900 --> 14:21.160
We look at all these overlapping mathematical descriptions on our detailed map

14:21.160 --> 14:24.620
and deliberately group together the ones that behave identically in reality.

14:24.920 --> 14:28.100
So we filter out the noise of our own mathematical language.

14:28.100 --> 14:28.580
Yes.

14:29.160 --> 14:34.940
And consider the underlying philosophical claim here, which Fliction calls witness invariance.

14:35.420 --> 14:38.300
Reality is what persists across different representations.

14:38.720 --> 14:39.100
Witness invariance.

14:39.780 --> 14:39.960
Right.

14:40.220 --> 14:44.340
If two quantum states look completely different in our flag space equation,

14:44.840 --> 14:48.400
but they yield the exact same physical measurement in a detector,

14:48.980 --> 14:53.220
what Fliction calls an admissible witness, then they are physically identical.

14:53.680 --> 14:54.200
Okay.

14:54.400 --> 14:55.060
I'm getting it.

14:55.060 --> 15:00.160
The standard complex numbers and Fliction's real flag space are really just two different

15:00.160 --> 15:04.340
mathematical nets draped over the exact same invariant physical reality.

15:04.540 --> 15:06.440
So the map is not the territory.

15:07.060 --> 15:11.780
The ontology, the actual true nature of reality, belongs only to the invariant structure.

15:12.040 --> 15:15.480
It's the thing that survives no matter what math you use to describe it.

15:15.500 --> 15:15.940
Exactly.

15:15.940 --> 15:21.100
And that underlying structure, Fliction argues, is geometric rotation and scaling, the amplet whist.

15:21.220 --> 15:24.380
So the quantum weirdness isn't a failure of our reality.

15:24.680 --> 15:28.700
It's a byproduct of us forgetting that our algebra was just a shorthand for geometry.

15:29.060 --> 15:29.820
Beautifully said.

15:30.100 --> 15:35.160
By uncompressing it, we realize the universe isn't playing a game of abstract algebraic dice.

15:35.380 --> 15:38.080
It is executing precise geometric rotations.

15:38.080 --> 15:43.980
Okay, so that handles the abstract, invisible, microscopic quantum realm.

15:44.320 --> 15:48.940
But it's one thing to say that subatomic math is secretly a geometric trick.

15:49.640 --> 15:59.360
Where this deep dive gets truly mind-altering for me is when Fliction applies this exact same amplet whist framework to the macro world.

15:59.360 --> 16:01.900
Oh yeah, this is where it gets really tangible.

16:02.220 --> 16:10.640
You're going to leave the theoretical quantum realm and literally zoom into the silicon circuitry inside the phone or the laptop you're listening to this on right now.

16:10.660 --> 16:12.060
Right, the stuff you can actually touch.

16:12.140 --> 16:12.520
Exactly.

16:13.040 --> 16:16.880
This is the domain of continuation geometry applied to electrical engineering.

16:17.140 --> 16:19.760
We are looking at how circuits process energy and information.

16:20.360 --> 16:26.080
And immediately, the source points out that the fundamental properties of circuits are, once again, amplet whist operators.

